    "body": "The shared-key point is underrated next to the failover story, but it's the one that actually bites teams at scale. \"One key's quota is everyone's ceiling, and revoking it breaks everyone at once\" — we lived that. The day you have to rotate a leaked key and realize six services embed it is the day you wish you'd put a gateway in front on day one, purely for the indirection. One push on the measured results: the 43% failure number is honest but assumes the app has no cross-provider fallback of its own, fair for a legacy stack. The subtler gateway win is that it makes the fallback uniform — every team gets the same retry semantics instead of each one hand-rolling backoff. Did you measure the cache hit rate on that 40-FAQ mix? On real support traffic the FAQ concentration is usually even higher than 8 distinct questions, and that's where the gateway pays for itself, not the failover.  "
